# Summary
Introduced a security control bypass: AuthenticatedUsers ACL grants are now accepted and grant access to all authenticated users, yet BlockPublicAcls enforcement still only checks AllUsers-based public flags. Explicit grants can therefore bypass public access blocking and expose data to any authenticated account.
The commit enables AuthenticatedUsers ACL grants and uses them in authorization checks, but the public ACL detection helpers still only consider AllUsers grants. When BlockPublicAcls is enabled, bucket and object ACL updates are rejected only if `public_read`/`public_write` are set. Because AuthenticatedUsers grants do not set those flags, an ACL XML/header grant can still be accepted and then authorize any authenticated account to read data. This undermines the public access block control for authenticated-user exposure.

Update: this report is stale against current `main`.
The current implementation treats `AuthenticatedUsers` as a public group for
`BlockPublicAcls` enforcement via `AclGrants::allows_public_groups(...)`, and
bucket/object ACL writes are rejected when explicit grants target either
`AllUsers` or `AuthenticatedUsers`.

