## Report
Attempted dynamic validation: `cargo test -p server-core put_bucket_acl_rejects_block_public_acls -- --nocapture` failed because the ec-sys build requires libisal (intel-isa-l) which is not installed in the container; valgrind and gdb are also unavailable. Code review shows: AuthenticatedUsers grants are modeled (AclGrantee::AuthenticatedUsers) and evaluated via AclGrants::allows_authenticated_users (crates/s3-types/src/lib.rs:368-487). Authorization uses requester_has_acl_permission, which accepts authenticated requesters (Requester.canonical_user_id present) and grants access if allows_authenticated_users is true (crates/server-core/src/coordinator.rs:4236-4244; Requester canonical_user_id at 1547-1583). Public ACL detection only checks AllUsers via acl_grants_public_read/write (coordinator.rs:4247-4252). BlockPublicAcls enforcement in bucket/object ACL updates only consults public_read/public_write derived from those helpers (coordinator.rs:7491-7522, 7561-7567). Explicit grant validation is a no-op (ensure_supported_bucket_acl_grants returns Ok at coordinator.rs:5795-5796), so AuthenticatedUsers grants can be set even when BlockPublicAcls is enabled, and then any authenticated requester is authorized—confirming the bypass. Canned AuthenticatedRead is blocked because BucketAcl::is_public includes AuthenticatedRead (coordinator.rs:1666-1678, 5622-5630), highlighting the gap for explicit grants.

